static void ApplyPatternData(ULyraRecoilProfile* Profile, const FLyraRecoilPatternData& Data)
{
    FArrayProperty* ArrayProp = FindFProperty<FArrayProperty>(ULyraRecoilProfile::StaticClass(), GET_MEMBER_NAME_CHECKED(ULyraRecoilProfile, PatternPoints));
    FIntProperty* LengthProp = FindFProperty<FIntProperty>(ULyraRecoilProfile::StaticClass(), GET_MEMBER_NAME_CHECKED(ULyraRecoilProfile, PatternLength));
    const bool bSizeChanged = Profile->PatternPoints.Num() != Data.Points.Num();

    Profile->PatternPoints = Data.Points;   // UPROPERTY array assignment
    Profile->PatternLength = Data.PatternLength;

    if (ArrayProp)
    {
        FPropertyChangedEvent Event(ArrayProp, bSizeChanged
            ? (Data.Points.Num() > 0 ? EPropertyChangeType::ArrayAdd : EPropertyChangeType::ArrayClear)
            : EPropertyChangeType::ValueSet);
        if (LengthProp) Event.SetActiveMemberProperty(LengthProp);
        Profile->PostEditChangeProperty(Event);
    }
}
```
Hmm, ActiveMemberProperty should be the array property's inner member? `SetActiveMemberProperty` is used for the *value* property within the changed container. For arrays, `MemberProperty` is set to the inner property typically. Setting it to LengthProp is wrong. Let me not call SetActiveMemberProperty, or set it to the array's Inner. Actually default MemberProperty == Property (the array property). Good enough; leave default.

Wait — `Profile->PatternPoints = Data.Points;` — direct assignment of TArray<FRecoilPatternPoint>. Fine.

